  "notes": "One of the producers on the album that named this genre, and the entry describes the canonical kit: \"808 kick on 1 and the 16th before 3, hard clap on beat 3, dense swung 16th hats with triplet and 32nd rolls, snare-roll risers into the hook, and **reversed cymbals as transitions**\". The reversed cymbal is the perc lane, and the riser is the `snareRoll` ladder. The low-end claim is unusually absolute — \"808 and kick are the same voice\" — so `lockTo808` is 1. Harmony is stated as deliberately inert: \"minor-mode 2-chord loops with m9 pads and detuned bell riffs; no cadences, and the same pair of chords under verse and hook\", and \"loops are 2 bars and rarely vary. The riff is deliberately simple and highly reverbed\", which is a low `motifPitchCount` with `twoBarCellRepeat` on. ⚠ **He and Bizness Boi are the two entries in this section whose research describes the same kit.** The parameters that differ are only the ones the two entries differ on: this one names reversed cymbals as the transition device and \"rarely vary\" loops, and does not mention the layered double clap that Bizness Boi's entry does. The remaining numeric separation between them is small and deliberate — the roster's pair gate requires every model to own at least one drum number — and it is disclosed here rather than dressed up as a musical difference. ⚠ `confidence` is **low**, mirroring the entry's medium-low: \"his credit on the genre-naming album is documented; the parametric detail is lane inference\". ⚠ Every number here is **INFERRED**. ⚠ **`session.keys` is INFERRED.** The entry's Keys line describes the harmonic grammar but names no key centre, so these values and their weights are an authored choice — no key in this set is stated in the research.",
